  [38;5;153mtype="module">[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m the clearest boundaries — [38;5;153mlogin[39m authenticates, [38;5;153mtrackEvent[39m records, the handler orchestrates. Each is testable
  alone, and swapping in a vendor touches only the transport.
  [3mCosts:[0m the handler must remember to call it, so a future second login path could silently skip tracking. Converting to
  ES modules is a real (small) change to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.

  [1mB. [38;5;153mlogin[39m emits its own event[0m
  [38;5;153mlogin[39m calls [38;5;153mtrackEvent[39m internally before returning.
  [3mBuys:[0m impossible to forget; every login is tracked by construction, and the handler stays as-is apart from [38;5;153mawait[39m.
  [3mCosts:[0m couples authentication to analytics. [38;5;153mlogin[39m now has two reasons to change and can't be tested without stubbing
  the tracker. For an auth function this coupling tends to age badly — analytics requirements churn far faster than auth
  does.

  [1mC. Event bus / pub-sub[0m
  [38;5;153mlogin[39m publishes [38;5;153mlogin_succeeded[39m to a small emitter; an analytics subscriber registered at startup consumes it.
  [3mBuys:[0m fully decoupled, and cheap to add more event types or more consumers later.
  [3mCosts:[0m genuine indirection — you can't tell what happens on login by reading the login path. For exactly one event
  with exactly one consumer, this is machinery bought against a future that may not arrive. YAGNI says no for now, and A
  upgrades to C later without rework if it does.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It gives you the seam you asked for with the fewest moving parts, keeps [38;5;153mlogin[39m honest as an auth
  function, and leaves both B's guarantee and C's flexibility available later. The one thing it gives up — the
  compile-time guarantee that tracking can't be forgotten — is worth very little in a codebase with exactly one login
  path.
